WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:09.825
This is the Korea Daily Brief from the CSIS Korea Chair, read by an AI voice. It's Wednesday, October 21.

2
00:00:09.825 --> 00:00:23.757
Three things today: Kim Yo Jong orders loudspeakers along the full inter-Korean border, South Korea's exports surge 105 percent, and Seoul pulls more staff from Kyiv as a rift with Ukraine deepens.

3
00:00:23.757 --> 00:00:34.365
On the loudspeakers: North Korea is now wiring its entire border for psychological warfare, and South Korea has already promised decisive retaliation.

4
00:00:34.365 --> 00:00:41.790
Tomorrow, National Assembly committee hearings continue across ministries as the national audit rolls on.

5
00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:52.600
First, Kim Yo Jong has ordered loudspeaker arrays activated along the entire inter-Korean border by the end of October. The announcement came on October 21.

6
00:00:52.600 --> 00:01:08.740
It follows a border inspection one day earlier, on October 20, when Kim, who is deputy director of the Korean Workers' Party Central Committee, visited southern border fortification sites alongside Marshal Pak Jong Chon, the Ministry of National Defense adviser.

7
00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:17.180
According to KCNA, they ordered troops to, quote, "counterattack immediately," end quote, if South Korean forces threatened their safety.

8
00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:23.217
The loudspeaker announcement is an escalation of an earlier threat Kim Yo Jong made on October 19.

9
00:01:23.217 --> 00:01:33.074
South Korea's Ministry of National Defense had already responded to that threat, vowing, in its words, "decisive retaliation" against North Korean provocations.

10
00:01:33.074 --> 00:01:37.201
Satellite imagery ties the physical infrastructure to the rhetoric.

11
00:01:37.201 --> 00:01:50.015
An analysis published by 38 North, written by Martyn Williams, presents satellite evidence of an intensive upgrade of North Korea's southern border fence happening at the same time as the loudspeaker threats.

12
00:01:50.015 --> 00:01:58.516
The construction is distinct from earlier wall-building, and the timing aligns directly with the Kim Yo Jong and Pak Jong Chon inspection.

13
00:01:58.516 --> 00:02:15.827
A separate analysis, published by War on the Rocks, argues that changes in North Korean propaganda are the most reliable signal of Pyongyang's shifting priorities, and that identifying what is new, and why, is how analysts avoid systematic errors when reading escalatory signaling.

14
00:02:15.827 --> 00:02:19.400
For now: loudspeakers along the full border, by month-end.

15
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:34.571
Second, South Korea's exports for the first 20 days of October surged 105 percent compared with the same period a year ago. The driver is record semiconductor shipments, according to customs data reported by Reuters.

16
00:02:34.571 --> 00:02:47.896
The won extended gains to a fresh two-plus-year high against the U.S. dollar on the strength of those numbers. The export boom lands just ahead of a critical decision by the Bank of Korea on interest rates.

17
00:02:47.896 --> 00:02:58.698
Ninety-three percent of bond experts surveyed forecast a rate freeze. The Bank of Korea's policy rate currently stands at 2.50 percent. Markets reflected the optimism.

18
00:02:58.698 --> 00:03:07.559
Seoul's main stock index, the KOSPI, surged more than 3 percent on the day. The Korea Exchange activated a buy-side sidecar on the index.

19
00:03:07.559 --> 00:03:19.655
Eased investor concerns over artificial intelligence infrastructure spending drove the rebound, reversing the previous session's tech-led selloff. Not all the corporate news was positive.

20
00:03:19.655 --> 00:03:28.322
Samsung Biologics reported a greater than 20 percent decline in third-quarter net income compared with the same period a year earlier.

21
00:03:28.322 --> 00:03:42.811
And AI data center firm Elice plunged 32 percent on its initial public offering debut before rebounding by the daily limit the following day, illustrating a volatile reassessment of the AI infrastructure sector by investors.

22
00:03:42.811 --> 00:03:54.195
The Bank of Korea also reported separately that South Korean companies' profitability and debt ratios improved in 2025, providing a baseline for assessing corporate resilience.

23
00:03:54.195 --> 00:04:02.604
Set against that: the share of non-regular workers among South Korean wage earners reached its highest level since 2003 in August.

24
00:04:02.604 --> 00:04:08.490
The headline, though, is the export figure: up 105 percent in the first 20 days of October.

25
00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:21.474
Third, South Korea has pulled additional diplomatic staff from its embassy in Kyiv, deepening a rupture with Ukraine over Seoul's disclosure of North Korean prisoners of war captured by Ukraine.

26
00:04:21.474 --> 00:04:32.711
The withdrawal follows the earlier recall of Seoul's ambassador to Ukraine. The dispute centers on the timing and manner of Seoul's announcement about the North Korean prisoners of war.

27
00:04:32.711 --> 00:04:45.832
The main opposition People Power Party called the withdrawal an, quote, "emotional response," end quote. European partners have voiced unease over the rift between Seoul and a North Atlantic Treaty Organization ally.

28
00:04:45.832 --> 00:04:59.074
The Korea JoongAng Daily reports that European governments are concerned the episode strains Seoul's broader effort to position itself as a security partner to NATO allies supporting Ukraine. At the same time, the U.S.

29
00:04:59.074 --> 00:05:05.391
Forces Korea commander, General Xavier Brunson, was making the case for the alliance's industrial depth.

30
00:05:05.391 --> 00:05:16.993
Speaking at an American Chamber of Commerce Korea luncheon on October 21, he told Korean and American leaders they are, quote, "an inseparable part of our combined defense system," end quote.

31
00:05:16.993 --> 00:05:25.375
Not suppliers, he said, but partners in deterrence. He also called for more frequent year-round combined drills with South Korea. The U.S.

32
00:05:25.375 --> 00:05:35.337
Ambassador to South Korea separately extended condolences following the death of a Korean Augmentation to the United States Army soldier, with three others injured.

33
00:05:35.337 --> 00:05:43.780
The Ukraine staff withdrawal deepens a rift with an ally at a moment Seoul is also managing turbulence in its relationship with Washington.

34
00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:46.057
Now to Pyongyang.

35
00:05:46.057 --> 00:06:03.597
KCNA carried no top articles in today's monitored feed, but state media's central activity is already in the lead story: KCNA led with Kim Yo Jong and Marshal Pak Jong Chon's joint inspection of southern border fortification sites on October 20 and the orders issued to troops.

36
00:06:03.597 --> 00:06:06.763
Kim Jong Un has not appeared publicly for one day.

37
00:06:06.763 --> 00:06:18.415
Meanwhile, North Korean state websites including KCNA, Rodong Sinmun, and the foreign ministry site remained offline for a second consecutive day in an unexplained simultaneous outage.

38
00:06:18.415 --> 00:06:28.610
The bottom line from the brief: the border fortification inspection and the website blackout are running in parallel, with no explanation offered for the outage.

39
00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:43.012
The rest of the news, quickly. On North Korea's naval ambitions: satellite imagery from Planet Labs and Airbus shows that North Korea's Nampho Shipyard missed its October deadline to launch a 5,000-ton naval destroyer.

40
00:06:43.012 --> 00:06:52.157
Hull sections are visible at two potential outdoor assembly areas, and construction of at least one larger warship is also underway in a separate area.

41
00:06:52.157 --> 00:07:03.058
On North Korea-China trade: China's customs data shows North Korea doubled exports of steelmaking-linked goods to China in September, alongside increased fuel imports from Beijing.

42
00:07:03.058 --> 00:07:10.507
Total North Korea-China trade reached nearly 300 million dollars in September, the second-highest monthly figure this year.

43
00:07:10.507 --> 00:07:28.494
On North Korea's dual-track strategy with its partners: an analysis in The Diplomat argues Pyongyang manages a deliberate division of labor, weapons and military cooperation with Russia, light industry and trade with China, ensuring the two relationships are complementary rather than competitive.

44
00:07:28.494 --> 00:07:38.124
On the New Yalu River Bridge: commercial vehicle traffic was confirmed by October 16 satellite imagery, one day after the bridge's official October 15 opening.

45
00:07:38.124 --> 00:07:49.812
On North Korea-Russia education ties: the countries' education ministers have agreed to expand artificial intelligence and language exchanges. A Russian-language center has opened in Pyongyang.

46
00:07:49.812 --> 00:08:02.954
Canada's top general, General Jennie Carignan, said lessons from training in Ukraine can help Seoul counter North Korea's tactics, and that North Korea is mastering new ways of war through its cooperation with Russia.

47
00:08:02.954 --> 00:08:12.704
In South Korean politics: an appeals court reduced the sentence of a former justice minister to 9 years in connection with the December 2024 martial law episode.

48
00:08:12.704 --> 00:08:21.486
A National Election Commission official was found dead after being questioned by a special counsel team investigating procurement irregularities.

49
00:08:21.486 --> 00:08:33.174
The Democratic Party is formally considering a parliamentary investigation into Supreme Court Chief Justice Cho Hee-dae, alongside ongoing impeachment discussion, accusing him of delay tactics.

50
00:08:33.174 --> 00:08:48.496
President Lee Jae-myung accepted the resignation of You Hong-june, director of the National Museum of Korea, after You submitted for display a piece of calligraphy by a colonial-era collaborator from his private collection without adequate verification.

51
00:08:48.496 --> 00:08:53.220
President Lee also called for aggressive police reform on the 81st Police Day.

52
00:08:53.820 --> 00:09:09.200
Gallup Korea, surveying from October 13 to 15, put presidential approval at 39 percent, up from the prior week. The Democratic Party stood at 36 percent, the People Power Party at 27 percent, both trending up.

53
00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:22.882
On Capitol Hill. A top U.S. envoy cited, quote, "turbulence," end quote, in the U.S.-South Korea alliance, pointing to divergent priorities on wartime operational control and burden-sharing.

54
00:09:22.882 --> 00:09:32.670
No Korea-related hearings are listed for today, but the National Assembly's national audit hearings continue tomorrow, October 22, across ministries.

55
00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:47.471
Beyond the region. South Korea's four largest defense contractors are carrying combined order backlogs approaching 120 trillion won, with additional Poland deals identified as the key variable for further growth.

56
00:09:47.471 --> 00:09:59.596
Separately, South Korea has opened a second track of nuclear reactor export talks with the Czech Republic, extending negotiations beyond the Dukovany plant to include Temelin Units 3 and 4.

57
00:09:59.596 --> 00:10:16.340
On the diplomatic front, South Korea and Japan held their second working-level meeting on artificial intelligence on October 21 in Seoul, with roughly 30 officials on each side covering AI safety, standards, intellectual property, and multilateral coordination.

58
00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:31.413
Finally, the markets. The dollar bought about 1,324 won on October 21, down about four-tenths of a percent on the day. The KOSPI closed at about 5,736, up about 3.3 percent.

59
00:10:31.413 --> 00:10:46.730
Brent crude stood at about 111 dollars a barrel, up about 1.9 percent. That's the Korea Daily Brief for Wednesday, October 21. The full text, with a link to every source, is in today's email.
